# Task 5 – Static Members and Object Life Cycle

## Objective
To understand static members and the life cycle of Java objects.

## Static Variables
The ticketCounter variable is static because it is shared by
all ParkingTicket objects. It generates unique ticket numbers.

## Static Methods
The getTicketCounter() method is static because it accesses
class-level data without requiring an individual ticket object.

## Object Life Cycle
A ParkingTicket object is created when a vehicle enters the parking
facility. When the ticket is no longer referenced, it becomes
eligible for garbage collection.

## Object Passing
Objects can be passed as arguments to methods and returned
from methods using reference variables.

## Alternative Concept
If the counter were an instance variable, each ParkingFacility
object could maintain a separate counter. This is useful when
ticket numbering must be independent for different facilities.

A shared static counter provides one counter for all instances
of the class.

## Complexity Analysis
Updating the counter takes O(1) time.
The counter requires O(1) space.
For n ticket objects, the tickets require O(n) total space.

## Conclusion
Static members are useful for shared data, such as ticket numbers,
while instance variables store information specific to each ticket.